= A
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Groups in Action}

= 1
{parent=2008/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}
{c}

The hypothesis is a <two-transitive group action>. Given $\alpha,\beta\ne\omega_1$, apply it to $(\omega_1,\alpha)$ and $(\omega_1,\beta)$. The resulting element fixes $\omega_1$ and sends $\alpha$ to $\beta$, so the <stabilizer subgroup> acts transitively on the remaining $n-1$ points.

The action on all of $\Omega$ is also transitive. By the <orbit-stabilizer theorem>,
$$
|G|=n|\operatorname{Stab}_G(\omega_1)|,\qquad
|\operatorname{Stab}_G(\omega_1)|=(n-1)|\operatorname{Stab}_G(\omega_1,\omega_2)|.
$$
Thus $\boxed{n(n-1)\mid |G|}$. The diagonal pairs form one orbit, while two-transitivity makes all ordered unequal pairs a second orbit. Equality is preserved by a group action, so these cannot mix. There are **exactly two orbits on $\Omega\times\Omega$**.

= B
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Introduction to Fields}

= 1
{parent=2008/B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}
{c}

<Eisenstein's criterion> says that an integer polynomial with leading coefficient not divisible by a prime $\ell$, all other coefficients divisible by $\ell$, and constant coefficient not divisible by $\ell^2$ is irreducible over the rationals, provided it is primitive; the usual <Gauss's lemma> supplies the passage from integer to rational factors.

Apply it with $\ell=2$ to
$$
(x+1)^8+1=x^8+8x^7+28x^6+56x^5+70x^4+56x^3+28x^2+8x+2.
$$
All nonleading coefficients are even and the constant is not divisible by four. A translation of the variable preserves reducibility, so **$x^8+1$ is irreducible over $\mathbb Q$**.

Let $\zeta=e^{i\pi/8}$ and $c=\cos(\pi/8)$. The minimal polynomial of $\zeta$ is $x^8+1$, so $[\mathbb Q(\zeta):\mathbb Q]=8$. Since $2c=\zeta+\zeta^{-1}$, the number $\zeta$ satisfies $t^2-2ct+1=0$ over $\mathbb Q(c)$. This real field cannot contain the nonreal $\zeta$, so that extension has degree exactly two. The <tower law for field extensions> gives
$$
\boxed{[\mathbb Q(\zeta):\mathbb Q(c)]=2,\qquad
[\mathbb Q(c):\mathbb Q]=4.}
$$

= C
{parent=2008}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Number Theory}

= 1
{parent=2008/C}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}
{c}

The <quadratic reciprocity> states that for distinct odd primes $a,b$,
$$
\left(\frac ab\right)\left(\frac ba\right)
=(-1)^{(a-1)(b-1)/4}.
$$
For fixed odd prime $r$, first suppose that $p,q\ne r$. The congruence $p\equiv q\pmod{4r}$ gives the same residues modulo $r$ and modulo four. Reciprocity yields
$$
\left(\frac rp\right)
=\left(\frac pr\right)(-1)^{(r-1)(p-1)/4}
=\left(\frac qr\right)(-1)^{(r-1)(q-1)/4}
=\left(\frac rq\right).
$$
If $p=r$, the congruence forces $r\mid q$, so $q=r$ and both symbols are zero; the same handles $q=r$.

For $n=\prod_jr_j^{a_j}$, where all $r_j$ are odd primes, $4r_j$ divides $4n$. Thus $p\equiv q\pmod{4n}$ implies equality of each prime-numerator <Legendre symbol>. Multiplicativity gives
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac np\right)
=\prod_j\left(\frac{r_j}p\right)^{a_j}
=\prod_j\left(\frac{r_j}q\right)^{a_j}
=\left(\frac nq\right).}
$$
This also covers symbols that vanish; for $n=1$ both sides are one.

= D
{parent=2008}
